WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.249
A valid signature can protect a token that
this API must reject.

2
00:00:05.249 --> 00:00:09.048
The issuer claim, called iss, identifies
the issuing authority.

3
00:00:09.048 --> 00:00:12.087
Configure the exact issuer values trusted
at this

4
00:00:12.087 --> 00:00:15.696
boundary and bind each issuer to its
verification keys.

5
00:00:15.696 --> 00:00:20.825
Finding any key that verifies a token does
not make its issuer acceptable.

6
00:00:20.825 --> 00:00:23.864
The audience claim, aud, identifies
intended recipients.

7
00:00:23.864 --> 00:00:26.714
Require the audience designated for this
API.

8
00:00:26.714 --> 00:00:29.373
The JWT format permits a single audience

9
00:00:29.373 --> 00:00:32.127
string or an array; use the library's

10
00:00:32.127 --> 00:00:34.977
documented matching behavior against
explicit configured values.

11
00:00:34.977 --> 00:00:38.016
A token issued for another service must
not

12
00:00:38.016 --> 00:00:41.625
pass merely because the same identity
provider signed it.

13
00:00:41.625 --> 00:00:45.614
Avoid substring matching or a policy that
accepts every audience.

14
00:00:45.614 --> 00:00:49.223
Time policy needs both presence checks and
value checks.

15
00:00:49.223 --> 00:00:53.212
Require expiration for access tokens and
reject them when expired.

16
00:00:53.212 --> 00:00:56.346
Respect the not-before claim when present,
and require

17
00:00:56.346 --> 00:00:59.196
it if your profile depends on it.

18
00:00:59.196 --> 00:01:01.855
JWT NumericDate values use seconds since
the

19
00:01:01.855 --> 00:01:04.799
Unix epoch, unlike JavaScript timestamps
in milliseconds.

20
00:01:04.799 --> 00:01:07.459
Let the library validate these values and

21
00:01:07.459 --> 00:01:10.403
apply only a small, justified clock
tolerance.

22
00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:14.392
Fix clock synchronization instead of
hiding problems with broad leeway.

23
00:01:14.392 --> 00:01:17.621
Issued at, or iat, records issuance time
and

24
00:01:17.621 --> 00:01:21.325
can support maximum-age policy; it does
not replace expiration.

25
00:01:21.325 --> 00:01:25.884
Subject, or sub, identifies a principal
according to the issuer's contract.

26
00:01:25.884 --> 00:01:28.733
Require the subject when constructing user
identity.

27
00:01:28.733 --> 00:01:31.583
The JWT identifier, jti, can identify a

28
00:01:31.583 --> 00:01:34.432
token but does not automatically prevent
replay.

29
00:01:34.432 --> 00:01:36.331
Rejecting repeat use requires an

30
00:01:36.331 --> 00:01:38.421
additional protocol or server-side
mechanism.

31
00:01:38.421 --> 00:01:40.510
Token purpose is another boundary.

32
00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:43.170
Access, refresh, email-verification,
password-recovery, and service

33
00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.639
tokens must have distinct acceptance
rules.

34
00:01:45.639 --> 00:01:48.298
An identity token intended for a client

35
00:01:48.298 --> 00:01:51.148
is not automatically an API access token.

36
00:01:51.148 --> 00:01:54.377
Use the issuer's documented profile,
including audience, explicit

37
00:01:54.377 --> 00:01:57.701
type, or a dedicated purpose claim as
appropriate.

38
00:01:57.701 --> 00:02:00.076
Validate the combination, not an isolated

39
00:02:00.076 --> 00:02:02.260
label, before trusting
application-specific claims.

